    <h1>California Website Privacy Policy Template (Free Sample)</h1>
    <p class="last-updated">Updated November 13, 2026</p>

    <p>A California privacy policy template is a privacy notice built around what the CCPA (as amended by the CPRA) and CalOPPA require a website to disclose: the personal information you collect, its sources, why you use it, who receives it, whether you sell or share it, how long you keep it, and how California residents use their rights. Copy the free sample below, fill in the brackets, and delete anything that doesn't describe your site.</p>

    <p class="note">This page describes what the statutes and California Privacy Protection Agency (CPPA) regulations say. It is not legal advice.</p>

    <nav class="toc">
      <p class="toc-title">Contents</p>
      <ol>
        <li><a href="#who-is-covered">Which Businesses the CCPA/CPRA Covers</a></li>
        <li><a href="#categories-table">The Notice at Collection and Categories Table</a></li>
        <li><a href="#consumer-rights">Consumer Rights and the Two Request Channels</a></li>
        <li><a href="#do-not-sell-or-share">"Do Not Sell or Share" and What Triggers It</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sample-template">Copy-Ready California Privacy Notice Template</a></li>
        <li><a href="#faq">FAQ</a></li>
      </ol>
    </nav>

    <h2 id="who-is-covered">Which Businesses the CCPA/CPRA Covers</h2>

    <p>The CCPA applies to a for-profit business that does business in California, collects consumers' personal information, and meets at least one threshold in Cal. Civ. Code §1798.140(d)(1):</p>

    <ul>
      <li>Annual gross revenue above <strong>$26,625,000</strong> in the preceding year. The statute says $25,000,000, adjusted every odd-numbered year under §1798.199.95(d); the CPPA set the current figure from January 1, 2025.</li>
      <li>Buys, sells, or shares the personal information of <strong>100,000 or more</strong> consumers or households a year.</li>
      <li>Derives <strong>50% or more</strong> of annual revenue from selling or sharing personal information.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>A "consumer" is any California resident (§1798.140(i)), including visitors, customers and, since January 1, 2023, employees and job applicants.</p>

    <p><strong>CalOPPA has no threshold.</strong> Cal. Bus. &amp; Prof. Code §22575 requires every commercial website collecting personally identifiable information from California residents to post a policy listing the categories collected and third-party recipients, any process for reviewing that data, how policy changes are announced, an effective date, how the site responds to "Do Not Track" signals, and whether other parties track visitors across sites. Unsure which laws apply? Try the <a href="/tools/privacy-law-checker/">privacy law applicability checker</a> or read <a href="/resources/do-i-need-a-privacy-policy-for-my-website/">whether your website needs a privacy policy</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="categories-table">The Notice at Collection and Categories Table, Column by Column</h2>

    <p>The <strong>notice at collection</strong> (§1798.100(a)) must state, at or before collection, the categories collected, the purposes, whether each is sold or shared, and how long each is kept. Online, it can be a link to the matching privacy policy section, placed where data is collected (11 CCR §7012). The <strong>privacy policy</strong> (§1798.130(a)(5)) must cover the past 12 months: categories collected, sources, purposes, categories of third parties, and separate lists of what was sold or shared and what was disclosed for a business purpose. It must be updated at least once every 12 months.</p>

    <p>One table usually covers both, one column per statutory question:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Category:</strong> the statutory names in §1798.140(v)(1)(A)–(L), such as identifiers, commercial information, or inferences.</li>
      <li><strong>Examples:</strong> the actual data points on your site.</li>
      <li><strong>Sources:</strong> the consumer, their browser or device, service providers, or ad partners.</li>
      <li><strong>Purpose:</strong> the business or commercial reason.</li>
      <li><strong>Disclosed to:</strong> categories of third parties.</li>
      <li><strong>Sold or shared?:</strong> yes or no per category.</li>
      <li><strong>Retention:</strong> a period, or the criteria for one.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Example rows for a small site with a contact form, payments, analytics and an ad pixel:</p>

    <div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Example CCPA categories table" tabindex="0">
      <table class="compare-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Category</th>
            <th scope="col">Examples</th>
            <th scope="col">Sources</th>
            <th scope="col">Purpose</th>
            <th scope="col">Disclosed to</th>
            <th scope="col">Sold or shared?</th>
            <th scope="col">Retention</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
